 "drivingQuestion": "From where is the father's obligation to redeem his son derived?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "source supplied from Exodus 34:20 and Numbers 18:15",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "kiddushin.29a.15",
   "daf": "29a",
   "sentenceId": 14,
   "he": "לִפְדּוֹתוֹ. מְנָלַן? דִּכְתִיב: ״כׇּל בְּכוֹר בָּנֶיךָ תִּפְדֶּה״. וְהֵיכָא דְּלָא פַּרְקֵיהּ אֲבוּהּ מִיחַיַּיב אִיהוּ לְמִפְרְקֵיהּ דִּכְתִיב: ״תִּפְדֶּה״ ״תִּפָּדֶה״.",
   "en": "§ The baraita teaches that a father is obligated «to redeem» his son. The Gemara asks: «From where do we» derive this? The Gemara answers that this is «as it is written: “All the firstborn of your sons you shall redeem”» (Exodus 34:20). «And» in a case «where» one’s «father does not redeem him he is obligated to redeem» himself, «as it is written: “You shall redeem [pado tifde]”» (Numbers 18:15). The repetition of the verb indicates that a firstborn is required to be redeemed in any case, even if his father neglects to do so."
  }
 ],
